"""Liam's stories: one-minute reads for a 10-year-old.

True sports stories are told as they actually happened. The famous versions
often aren't quite right, and where the myth differs from the facts, the story
says so (Jordan was left on JV, not cut from the school team).

Each: (title, tags, story, takeaway, meaning, question)
"""

STORIES = [
("The Kid Who Didn't Make Varsity", ["basketball"],
"In 1978, a skinny sophomore at Laney High School in North Carolina tried out for the varsity basketball team. When the list went up, his name wasn't on it. He'd been kept on the junior varsity team instead. One of the spots went to a taller player, because the varsity team needed height.\n\nThe boy went home and was upset. He could have decided basketball wasn't for him.\n\nInstead, he got to the gym early. He stayed late. He played on JV and became its best player, scoring so much that people came just to watch him. That summer he grew, and he kept practicing.\n\nThe next year he made varsity. A few years later he hit the winning shot in a college championship game. The boy's name was Michael Jordan.\n\nYears later, Jordan said that whenever he felt tired in practice, he'd think about that list without his name on it, and it would push him to keep going.",
"Not making the team isn't the end of the story. It can be the start of it.",
"People often say Jordan was \"cut\" from his high-school team. He wasn't cut from the school. He was left on JV. It's still a big disappointment, and what matters is what he did next: he used it to push himself.",
"What's a disappointment that could turn into your \"list on the wall\"?"),

("The Napkin Contract", ["soccer"],
"When Lionel Messi was about 10, doctors found that his body wasn't making enough of the hormone that helps kids grow. There was a medicine that could help, but it was very expensive, and his family in Argentina couldn't keep paying for it.\n\nMessi was already amazing with a ball. So when he was 13, he and his dad flew to Spain for a tryout with FC Barcelona. The coaches couldn't believe what they saw: this tiny kid was dribbling past everyone.\n\nBut the club took a while to decide. Finally, a Barcelona director named Carles Rexach grabbed the nearest thing he could write on, a paper napkin, and wrote an agreement on it to sign Messi and help with his treatment.\n\nMessi moved to Spain, far from his friends. It was hard. But he kept playing, kept growing, and became one of the greatest players ever. That napkin became so famous that in 2024 it was sold at an auction for hundreds of thousands of dollars.",
"Being small, or starting behind, doesn't decide how far you can go.",
"Messi needed help to grow. He also needed people who believed in him, and he had to be brave enough to move far from home. A lot of hard things had to go right, and he worked for all of it.",
"Who has believed in you? How could you thank them?"),

("1 in 5,000", ["soccer"],
"Before the 2015-16 season, the experts who make predictions said Leicester City had about a 1 in 5,000 chance of winning the Premier League. Almost impossible. The season before, Leicester had spent months at the bottom of the table and only just avoided being sent down to a lower league.\n\nLeicester didn't have big stars. Their striker, Jamie Vardy, had been playing for a small non-league team a few years earlier while working in a factory.\n\nBut they worked incredibly hard for each other. They defended together and attacked fast. Vardy scored in 11 games in a row, a Premier League record. Every week people said, \"They'll fall apart soon.\" Every week they won again.\n\nIn May 2016, Leicester City were champions of England. Some people still call it the biggest surprise in sports history.",
"A team that works for each other can beat teams with bigger names.",
"Leicester didn't have the most money or the most famous players. They had a plan, they trusted each other, and they kept going every single week, even when everyone said it wouldn't last.",
"When have you been told something was impossible?"),

("The Miracle on Ice", ["more"],
"At the 1980 Winter Olympics in Lake Placid, New York, the US hockey team was made up of college players. Most of them were about 20 years old.\n\nTheir opponent in the medal round was the Soviet Union, the best team in the world. The Soviets had won the last four Olympic gold medals. Just before the Olympics, they'd beaten the Americans 10-3.\n\nThe US coach, Herb Brooks, told his players they were born to be there. The game was close all the way. With ten minutes left, US captain Mike Eruzione scored to make it 4-3. The Americans held on as the crowd counted down the final seconds.\n\nThe TV announcer shouted, \"Do you believe in miracles? Yes!\"\n\nTwo days later, the US beat Finland to win the gold medal.",
"On any given day, the underdog can win, if they believe it.",
"The Soviets were better on paper. The Americans were younger and less experienced. But a single game isn't played on paper, and a team that believes it can win plays braver.",
"When has a team you were on surprised everyone?"),

("The Promise", ["soccer"],
"In 1950, the World Cup final round was played in Brazil. Everyone was sure Brazil would win the last game against Uruguay. But Uruguay came back and won 2-1, and the whole country was heartbroken.\n\nIn a small town, a 9-year-old boy found his father crying next to the radio. The boy had never seen his dad cry before. He told him, \"Don't cry, Dad. One day I'll win the World Cup for you.\"\n\nEight years later, that boy was 17 years old and playing for Brazil at the 1958 World Cup in Sweden. He scored a hat-trick in the semi-final and two goals in the final. Brazil won their first World Cup.\n\nThe boy's name was Pelé. He told this story himself, many times.",
"Big promises are kept with years of small practices.",
"Pelé was just a little kid when he made that promise. He kept it by playing every day, often with a ball made of socks stuffed with paper, because his family couldn't afford a real one.",
"What's a promise you'd like to keep one day?"),

("The Header in the 122nd Minute", ["soccer"],
"At the 2011 Women's World Cup, the USA played Brazil in the quarter-finals. The USA had a player sent off, so they had to play with only ten. The game went to extra time, and Brazil scored to go ahead 2-1.\n\nThe clock ticked past 120 minutes. The game should have been over. Then Megan Rapinoe got the ball on the left side and sent a long, high cross toward the far post.\n\nAbby Wambach ran, jumped and headed it into the net. The score was 2-2 in the 122nd minute, one of the latest goals in World Cup history.\n\nThe USA went on to win the penalty shootout.",
"Keep playing until the whistle. Really.",
"Plenty of teams would have given up down to ten players, losing in extra time. The USA kept attacking until the very last second, and that's why they had a chance at all.",
"Have you ever kept going when it seemed too late?"),

("Finishing the Race", ["more"],
"At the 1992 Olympics in Barcelona, a British runner named Derek Redmond was running the 400 meters semi-final. He'd trained for years for this race.\n\nHalfway around the track, he felt a terrible pain in his leg. A muscle in the back of it had torn. He fell to the track.\n\nThe other runners finished. But Derek got up. He began hopping toward the finish line, crying with pain.\n\nThen a man pushed past the security guards and ran onto the track. It was Derek's dad, Jim. He put his arm around his son and told him they would finish the race together.\n\nLeaning on his dad, Derek limped to the finish. The whole stadium, 65,000 people, stood up and cheered.",
"Finishing matters, even when you can't win. And you don't have to do hard things alone.",
"Derek didn't win the race. He didn't even get a time. But people still remember him more than many of the runners who did win, because of how he finished.",
"Who helps you when things go wrong?"),

("The Tiny Country", ["soccer"],
"Iceland is a small country near the Arctic, with only about 330,000 people. That's fewer than live in many American cities. It's cold and dark for much of the year, so the country built indoor fields where kids could play soccer all winter.\n\nIn 2016, Iceland played in the European Championship for the first time. One of their coaches was a part-time dentist!\n\nNobody expected much. But Iceland didn't lose a group game. In the next round they played England, a country of millions of people with famous stars. Iceland won 2-1.\n\nTheir fans did a Viking war chant: a slow, huge clap together, \"HUH!\" The whole world copied it.",
"Being small isn't the same as being weak.",
"Iceland didn't have more players. They had good coaching, lots of practice, and a team that played like a family.",
"What's something small that turned out to be mighty?"),

("Pick Number 199", ["football"],
"In the 2000 NFL Draft, 198 players were picked before a skinny quarterback from the University of Michigan. At the start of the draft, he'd run a slow 40-yard dash and looked like he had no muscles at all.\n\nThe New England Patriots finally picked him in the sixth round, number 199. He was one of four quarterbacks on the team, and in his first season he barely played.\n\nHe worked harder than anyone, studied game film, and kept waiting for a chance. In his second season the starting quarterback got hurt, and the backup went in.\n\nHe led the Patriots to the Super Bowl that season and won it. By the time he retired, Tom Brady had won seven Super Bowls, more than any player in history.",
"Being picked last doesn't mean you'll finish last.",
"198 picks went by. Brady could have decided that meant he wasn't good enough. He decided to prove them wrong, and he got ready so that when his chance came, he could take it.",
"How can you get ready now for a chance that might come later?"),

("The Fastest Woman", ["more"],
"Wilma Rudolph was born in Tennessee in 1940, one of 22 children. She was very sick as a little girl and got polio, a disease that weakened her left leg. Doctors said she might never walk normally.\n\nShe wore a heavy metal brace on her leg. Her family took turns massaging her leg every day. She did her exercises again and again.\n\nBy the time she was 12, she could walk without the brace. Then she started running, and it turned out she was fast. Very fast.\n\nAt the 1960 Olympics in Rome, Wilma Rudolph won three gold medals and was called the fastest woman in the world.",
"Where you start isn't where you have to finish.",
"Wilma's family helped her every single day for years. Then she did the running herself. Big comebacks usually have a whole team behind them.",
"What's something you couldn't do before that you can do now?"),

("108 Years", ["baseball"],
"The Chicago Cubs won the World Series in 1908. Then they didn't win it again. Not in 1920, not in 1950, not in 2000. Fans joked that the team was cursed. Grandparents waited their whole lives and never saw a Cubs championship.\n\nIn 2016, the Cubs made it to the World Series against Cleveland. It went to Game 7. The score was tied after nine innings. Then it started to rain, and the game stopped.\n\nDuring the short rain delay, outfielder Jason Heyward gathered the whole team in a small room and gave them a speech: stay together, keep fighting, and keep believing in each other.\n\nWhen the game started again, the Cubs scored two runs in the 10th inning and won 8-7. After 108 years, the Cubs were champions.",
"A few words at the right moment can change a whole team.",
"Heyward wasn't having a great series himself. He still stood up and helped his teammates believe. Being a leader isn't about being the best player that day.",
"When could you say something to help your team?"),

("Jesse and Luz", ["more"],
"At the 1936 Olympics in Berlin, Germany, the American runner Jesse Owens entered the long jump. His biggest rival was a German jumper named Luz Long.\n\nOwens won the gold medal. Long won silver.\n\nThe first person to congratulate Owens was Luz Long. In front of a huge crowd, the two of them walked around the stadium together, arm in arm, talking and laughing.\n\nThey became friends and wrote letters to each other for years. Jesse Owens won four gold medals in Berlin, and later said the friendship with Luz Long meant more to him than any of them.",
"You can compete hard against someone and still be their friend.",
"Luz lost, but he was the first to celebrate the winner. That's what being a good sport really looks like.",
"How do you act when someone beats you?"),

# ─── Fables ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
("The Tortoise and the Hare", ["more"],
"A hare loved telling everyone how fast he was. \"Nobody can beat me,\" he bragged.\n\nA tortoise said quietly, \"I'll race you.\"\n\nThe hare laughed so hard he fell over. But the race was set.\n\nAt \"Go!\" the hare shot off and was soon far ahead. He looked back and couldn't even see the tortoise. \"This is too easy,\" he said. \"I'll take a nap.\"\n\nThe tortoise kept going. Step, step, step. He didn't stop, and he didn't speed up. He just kept going.\n\nThe hare woke up and saw the tortoise almost at the finish line. He sprinted as fast as he could, but it was too late. The tortoise won.",
"Steady and focused beats fast and lazy.",
"The hare was faster, but he stopped trying because he thought he'd already won. The tortoise kept going the whole way. Being talented isn't enough if you stop working.",
"Are you more like the tortoise or the hare, and when?"),

("The Bundle of Sticks", ["more"],
"An old farmer had sons who were always arguing. One day he called them together and handed them a bundle of sticks tied tightly with string.\n\n\"Break it,\" he said.\n\nEach son tried. They pushed, they pulled, they used their knees. Nobody could break the bundle.\n\nThen the farmer untied the string and gave each son one stick. \"Now try.\" Snap, snap, snap. Each stick broke easily.\n\n\"When you stick together,\" said the farmer, \"nobody can break you. When you fight each other, you're easy to beat.\"",
"A team that sticks together is hard to beat.",
"One stick is weak. The same sticks together are strong. That's why teams practice together, celebrate together and don't blame each other.",
"How can you help your team stick together?"),

("The Lion and the Mouse", ["more"],
"A lion was sleeping when a little mouse ran across his nose. The lion woke up and grabbed the mouse with his huge paw.\n\n\"Please let me go!\" squeaked the mouse. \"One day I might help you.\"\n\nThe lion laughed. How could a tiny mouse ever help a lion? But he let him go.\n\nSome days later, the lion was caught in a hunter's net. He roared and pulled, but he couldn't get free.\n\nThe little mouse heard him. He ran over and chewed through the ropes, one by one, until the lion was free.\n\n\"You laughed at me,\" said the mouse. \"But even a mouse can help a lion.\"",
"Be kind to everyone. The smallest player might save the day.",
"The lion thought the mouse was too small to matter. He was wrong. Everyone on a team has something to give.",
"Who's someone people overlook, who might surprise them?"),

("The Ant and the Grasshopper", ["more"],
"All summer, the grasshopper played and sang in the sun. The ant worked hard, carrying food back to his home one piece at a time.\n\n\"Why work so hard?\" laughed the grasshopper. \"Come and play!\"\n\n\"I'm getting ready for winter,\" said the ant.\n\nThen winter came. Snow covered everything. The ant was warm and had plenty to eat. The grasshopper was cold and hungry.\n\nHe knocked on the ant's door. The kind ant shared some food, but he said, \"Next summer, get ready for winter too.\"",
"Getting ready now makes the hard times easier later.",
"Practice is like the ant's summer. It doesn't always feel exciting, but when the big game comes, you'll be ready.",
"What are you getting ready for?"),

("The Boy Who Cried Wolf", ["more"],
"A boy's job was to watch the village sheep. It was boring. So one day, for fun, he shouted, \"Wolf! Wolf!\"\n\nThe villagers ran up the hill to help. There was no wolf. The boy laughed.\n\nA few days later he did it again. \"Wolf!\" Again everyone came running. Again there was no wolf.\n\nThen one evening a real wolf came. The boy shouted, \"Wolf! Wolf! Please help!\"\n\nBut nobody came. They thought he was joking again.",
"If you don't tell the truth, people stop believing you when it matters.",
"Trust is like a savings account. Every true thing you say puts some in. Every fib takes some out.",
"Why does trust matter on a team?"),

("The Crow and the Pitcher", ["more"],
"A thirsty crow found a pitcher with a little water at the bottom. But the pitcher was tall and narrow, and his beak couldn't reach the water.\n\nHe tried to tip it over. It was too heavy.\n\nThen he had an idea. He picked up a small pebble and dropped it in. Then another. And another. With every pebble, the water rose a little.\n\nAfter lots of pebbles, the water was high enough. The crow had his drink.",
"When you're stuck, think of a new way. Little by little gets it done.",
"The crow didn't give up when the first idea failed. He tried something else, and he was patient. One pebble didn't do it. Lots of them did.",
"What problem could you solve one pebble at a time?"),

("The Fox and the Grapes", ["more"],
"A hungry fox saw some juicy grapes hanging high on a vine. He jumped for them and missed. He jumped again and missed again. He jumped and jumped until he was tired.\n\nFinally he walked away with his nose in the air. \"Those grapes were probably sour anyway,\" he said.",
"Don't pretend you didn't want something just because it was hard to get.",
"The fox said the grapes were sour so he wouldn't feel bad. It's more honest to say, \"I didn't get it this time,\" and then figure out how to get better, like finding a longer stick.",
"When have you said you \"didn't care\" about something you actually did care about?"),

("The North Wind and the Sun", ["more"],
"The North Wind and the Sun argued about who was stronger. They saw a traveler walking down the road in a coat.\n\n\"Whoever can get his coat off is the strongest,\" said the Sun.\n\nThe Wind went first. He blew and blew as hard as he could. But the colder it got, the tighter the traveler held his coat.\n\nThen the Sun came out and shone gently. It got warmer and warmer. Soon the traveler took off his coat and sat in the shade.",
"Kindness often works better than force.",
"The Wind tried to force it. The Sun was patient and warm. With friends, teammates and even brothers and sisters, being kind usually gets further than being pushy.",
"When has being kind worked better than being bossy?"),
]
